# Headers for files served straight from the asset layer.
#
# Read by Cloudflare Workers static assets (and `wrangler dev`); the file itself
# is never served. Rules here apply ONLY to static files. That includes every
# website page: each one is a static HTML file written at build time
# (dist/index.html, dist/pricing.html … — see src/marketing/seo-head.ts). A
# response the Worker generates (the API, robots.txt, sitemap.xml) carries its
# own headers (see server.ts and server/platform.ts).
#
# The three inspection pages are development tools that happen to be published:
# brand-check.html draws the brand mark, icon-sheet.html draws every icon, and
# render-probe.html exercises canvas output. They are useful to a maintainer and
# meaningless to a visitor, so crawlers are told to skip them — the same job
# robots.txt does for the app, but this one also covers crawlers that ignore
# robots.txt, and it keeps them out of the index without hiding the pages.
#
# Each page is listed twice on purpose. Cloudflare's asset layer serves
# /brand-check from brand-check.html and 307-redirects the explicit .html URL,
# so the extensionless path is the one a crawler actually resolves; the .html
# rule is what a static server that keeps the extension (a bare `dist/`, a
# local preview) needs. tests/seo.test.ts holds both to the files on disk.

# PayPal's approval window is a popup that reports the result back through
# `window.opener`. Without this header the browser severs that relationship as
# soon as the window opens: the window appears, the checkout never completes,
# and it is indistinguishable from a dead button.
#
# `same-origin-allow-popups` keeps every isolation `same-origin` gives a page
# from a cross-origin *opener*, and only preserves the link to windows the page
# opens itself. It is set on every path, not just the checkout pages, because a
# checkout can be started from the account modal on any marketing page.
#
# This covers the pages the asset layer serves. A response the Worker builds
# itself sets the same header in server.ts; without this rule the static
# documents — every marketing page, the app shell — carried none at all.

/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups

# Repeated on every document rule below, deliberately.
#
# This catch-all already names every path, but each standalone page also has
# its own rule for Cache-Control, and whether the asset layer merges a specific
# rule with the catch-all or replaces it is not something this file can assert
# from here. If it replaces, every page carrying its own rule — /pricing
# included, which is where a checkout is started — was served with no
# Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy at all, and the browser severed window.opener as
# PayPal's approval window opened. That is indistinguishable from a dead
# button. Naming the header on each document rule makes the intended value
# present either way, and costs nothing where the rules do merge.

/brand-check
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

/brand-check.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

/icon-sheet
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

/icon-sheet.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

/render-probe
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

/render-probe.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

# The Worker also answers on Cloudflare's own address,
# scenering.<account>.workers.dev, with exactly the same pages as
# scenering.com. Only scenering.com should be listed in search results, so
# every page on a workers.dev address says noindex. Cloudflare adds this header
# to its versioned preview addresses by itself, but not to the main workers.dev
# address; this rule covers both. scenering.com and www.scenering.com do not
# match it. (Each page's canonical URL names https://scenering.com as well.)

https://:version.:subdomain.workers.dev/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

# The app shell names hashed JavaScript files, so it must be revalidated after
# every deployment. Otherwise an already-tested feature can remain pinned to
# yesterday's bundle even though the new branch preview finished deploying.
/
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups

/app
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups

/app/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups

# Every standalone page is its own HTML document, written at build time, naming
# the same hashed bundle (dist/features.html, dist/pricing.html …). Cloudflare's
# asset layer serves each one at its clean URL — /features, /pricing — so a rule
# for /index.html alone never covered them.
#
# That gap broke the features page in production. A deployment replaces the
# hashed bundle; a browser still holding yesterday's features.html asked for
# yesterday's JavaScript, got a 404, and rendered a blank document — the home
# page kept working because "/" was already revalidating. Every page now carries
# the same no-cache rule, so a document is never older than the bundle it names.

